From 9617bf0eaccd8d6842ba7879ebc0b3a49bc7bc75 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timothy Schmidt <timschmidt@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 06:27:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Close holes when dilation crosses their medial axis

Positive region offsets used the raw boundary walk for every region. A
hole whose inward parallels cross keeps its orientation after trimming,
so winding regularization reported a surviving hole: annulus, square,
slot and cut-corner holes stayed open past their half-width, a dumbbell
hole's channel never closed, and an annulus dilated by exactly its hole
radius failed with a topology error. These were wrong answers.

Regions with holes now apply the exact boundary bands by union, as
contraction applies them by difference. Exact certificates keep the
cheaper walk for holes that provably survive: one supporting circle with
r^2 > d^2, or a strictly convex polygon whose shifted edges all keep
positive length; undecided signs select the bands. Measured surviving
circular, square and annular cases stay within noise of the walk.

Seven witnesses in tests/disappearance_chains.rs compare against
independently authored exact regions; four fail on the previous source.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
